{#
    A code block, coloured on the server.

    Two things are decided here and both are in `guides-theme/GAPS.md`.

    **The colour is the server's.** `guides-code` highlights with a PHP port of
    highlight.js, so what lands in the page already carries `hljs-` classes —
    the ones `components.css` maps onto this system's three syntax colours. A
    reader with no JavaScript sees a coloured block, which is the point of a
    generator that ships HTML.

    **The language may be missing, and that must not be fatal.** A fenced block
    in Markdown can carry no language at all, and the Markdown parser leaves it
    null rather than falling back to the configured default. The highlighter's
    filter declares a string, so without the `default` here the whole render
    dies on the first bare fence with a TypeError three packages deep. `text`
    escapes the block and colours nothing, which is the honest answer when
    nobody said what it is.

    The caption sits above the block and inside the element, where this system
    already puts it — see `sds-code`. It is written between the tags rather
    than handed to the `caption` attribute because a caption node carries
    markup, and an attribute is a string.
#}
{% if node.raw %}
    {{ node.value|raw }}
{%- else -%}
    {#- The element, not a hand-built frame.

        `sds-code` takes what it is given and, when the colour is already
        there, hands it back untouched — wrapper and all, so the language, the
        starting line and the emphasised lines survive. What it adds is the
        head and the copy button, which is the whole reason to use an element
        rather than a `<pre>`.

        Before it upgrades — and on a page with no JavaScript at all — the
        caption and the `<code>` inside are drawn by `document.css` under
        `:not(:defined)`. The block reads either way; only the button is
        missing.

        The caption and the block are written with nothing between them, and
        that is not formatting: the element lifts its children out and copies
        what it finds to the clipboard, so a newline here would land in front
        of the first line of every pasted block. -#}
    <sds-code code-lang="{{ node.language|default('text') }}" copy
        {%- if node.classes %} class="{{ node.classesString }}"{% endif -%}
        >{%- if node.caption %}<div class="sds-code__caption">{{ renderNode(node.caption) }}</div>{% endif -%}
        <code class="language-{{ node.language|default('text') }}{{ node.startingLineNumber ? ' line-numbers' }}"
        {%- if node.startingLineNumber %} data-start="{{ node.startingLineNumber }}"{% endif -%}
        {%- if node.emphasizeLines %} data-emphasize-lines="{{ node.emphasizeLines }}"{% endif -%}
    >{{- node.value|highlight(node.language|default('text')) -}}</code></sds-code>
{%~ endif -%}
